version 0.1
model "merchant-storage-depth"
time calendar daily from 2026-01-01 for 365

// DEPTH: how much of a cycle is worth running today, not merely whether one is.
//
// A battery does not choose between a full cycle and nothing. Each MWh it
// moves is a separate decision: discharge the k-th dearest hour only if that
// hour beats what the marginal charging hour costs, grossed up for the
// round-trip loss, plus the wear of the cycle. Beyond some k it stops paying,
// and that k is the DEPTH.
//
// This is what a quantile is for. `quantile_of` inverts a price into the share
// of hours above it, which is exactly "how much of the day clears the bar".
//
// TWO AXES AGAIN. The `curve` carries the day's LEVEL; the `quantile` carries
// the intraday SHAPE, each hour as a ratio to its own day's mean. Depth varies
// across days because the wear is a fixed $/MWh while the spread scales with
// the level — so on a cheap day fewer hours clear it. With zero wear the level
// cancels and every day runs full, which is the correct answer to a question
// nobody would ask.

entity asset battery {
  lifecycle facility
}
entity party operator { name = "Operator" }

assume power_mw   = 20.0
assume eff_ac_dc  = 0.96
assume eff_dc_ac  = 0.96
assume eff_cell   = 0.9757
assume round_trip = inputs.eff_ac_dc * inputs.eff_dc_ac * inputs.eff_cell
assume usable_mwh = 64.0
assume charge_mwh = inputs.usable_mwh / inputs.round_trip

// The share of a day the asset can occupy, from its own duration.
assume dis_share  = inputs.usable_mwh / inputs.power_mw / 24.0
assume chg_share  = inputs.charge_mwh / inputs.power_mw / 24.0

// Degradation, per MWh discharged. This is what makes depth a real question.
assume cycle_cost_mwh = 0.0

curve day_price {
